---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const compCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Beyond national walls', note: 'Comparative literature reads ACROSS languages and borders: Goethe coined "Weltliteratur" (world literature, 1827) predicting an age of international exchange. The field studies how texts travel, translate and transform.' },
    { math: '2 · Influence and parallel', note: 'Does a text descend from another (influence) or arise independently (parallel)? tracking motifs (the flood, the doomed lovers, the trickster) across cultures distinguishes borrowing from convergent evolution.' },
    { math: '3 · Translation is interpretation', note: 'Every translation makes hundreds of decisions (Homer\'s "wine-dark sea" — is the sea really wine-dark?). Eco, Babel: "the language of translation" — the field treats translations as readings worth analysing in their own right.' },
    { math: '4 · World literature now', note: 'Moretti\'s "distant reading": patterns across thousands of texts, not close reading of a few — controversial, productive. The circulation question: which books cross borders (detached, portable ones) and which stay local (rooted in specific places)?' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/expert/comparative-literature">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative literature reads <strong>across borders: how texts travel, translate, influence
    and fail to travel</strong>. Born in the 19th century with Goethe's "world literature" dream,
    it now studies global circulation — including why some stories go universal and some stay
    gloriously rooted.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="world literature, influence, translation, distant reading" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />

  <h2>Methods and controversies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The translation debate:</strong> Venuti's "foreignisation" (keep the foreignness visible) vs "domestication" (smooth into the target language) — every translation is a political choice about how foreign to let the foreign remain.</li>
    <li><strong>Postcolonial comparative work:</strong> reading empire's languages against empire — Achebe writing English that carries Igbo syntax; Rushdie's "chutnified" English. The former colony answering in the coloniser's tongue, changed.</li>
    <li><strong>The shrinking/hypothesis:</strong> Damrosch's definition — world literature is not a canon but a MODE of reading: any work becomes world literature when it gains from circulation beyond its origin.</li>
    <li><strong>Distant reading's promise and peril:</strong> Moretti's quantitative sweeps find patterns (genre rise/fall, plot shapes via AI analysis of 2,000+ plots) that close reading cannot — and miss what close reading catches. The field's current dialectic.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goethe's conversations with Eckermann (1827); Damrosch, What Is World Literature? (2003); Moretti, Graphs, Maps, Trees (2005); Venuti, The Translator's Invisibility.</p>
</TopicPage>
